# base_unittests tests that do not pass on Jam OS yet (tools/cr-test.sh base,
# tools/cr/base-run.py). A "[layer] reason" line, then the full names of the
# tests it explains, one a line (a name may use * as the wildcard). Or one
# line "<pattern> <layer> <reason>". The tests are not run (CR_BASE_ALL=1
# runs them anyway and reports a failure as known), so a `base` PASS means
# every other test passed.
#
# Made 2026-10-16 from CR_BASE_ALL=1 runs of the shards of the suites on
# base_unittests relinked against main's libc (tools/cr/base-run.py: each
# suite a program of its own; tests that crashed alone). The layer is the
# track that owns the fix: posix (libc and its call layer), proc (/proc
# files), fs (a file system under /data: extfs, or the mapping calls),
# launch (starting programs: CB5's launcher patch), jit (executable memory
# by capability), toolchain (the link), musl (musl's own behaviour: the
# same on Linux with musl). A cause marked "?" is a guess from the failing call.
# Findings with their evidence: docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB2 as built" and "CB2b on ext".
#
# The list is for an ext /data (CB2b, 2026-10-16: the 44 file-system tests that
# only a FAT /data fails are in base_unittests.fat.skip, which base-run.py adds
# when the stick's /data is FAT).
#
# Shrunk 2026-10-17 (CB-RM) from CR_BASE_ALL=1 CR_BASE_LISTED=1 runs of base_unittests
# relinked against main 0fa8eea5 (fork, EFAULT, CBF2a/2b/4, ext limits): what
# passed came off, 596 listed became 259 (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB-RM"). The run's
# death tests use gtest's fast style (base-run.py --death-style): base's own
# threadsafe style execve()s the program in the clone child, which waits for FL3.

[posix] death tests that still fail with gtest's fast style (the harness default: the child is a fork, FL1b and FL2; 275 of the 281 CB2 listed pass that way). StackDumpSignalHandlerIsMallocFree: the forked child dies of SIGSEGV but its stack trace never reaches the captured stderr (the child's stderr is a file reopened by path at its first use, FL2; why the handler writes nothing is not found). The other four set the threadsafe style themselves: gtest's clone child runs execve on a two-page stack, and the layer's execve (path_get has 4 KiB buffers) overflows it, so the child dies of "page fault at rip <__jl_path_get>" and prints no "Check failed": FL3 (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB-RM"). With CR_BASE_DEATH=threadsafe (base's own default) 145 of the 281 fail that way and 125 pass only because their matcher accepts any death
SequenceCheckerTest.MoveOffSequenceBanned
StackTraceDeathTest.StackDumpSignalHandlerIsMallocFree
ThreadCheckerTest.MoveOffThreadBanned
WeakPtrDeathTest.ArrowOperatorChecksOnBadDereference
WeakPtrDeathTest.StarOperatorChecksOnBadDereference

[posix] inotify_init and inotify_init1 (syscalls 253 and 294) are ENOSYS: no file watching
FilePathWatcher*

[launch] base::LaunchProcess and the multi-process test children: fork works (FL1b, FL2) but the forked child's execvp fails (it prints "LaunchProcess: failed to execvp:" and exits 127; no kernel line and no ENOSYS line, the errno is not logged): execve in a forked child is FL3's. 15 of these pass anyway, vacuously (the test never reads the child's exit status, or looks before the child has failed: ProcessUtilTest.SpawnChild, ProcessTest.Terminate, ...), and stay listed so FL3's CR_BASE_ALL=1 rerun checks them for real. SharedMemorySwitchTest.PassViaSwitch, HistogramSharedMemoryTest.PassSharedMemoryRegion_Enabled and FieldTrialListTest.PassFieldTrialSharedMemoryOnCommandLine passed in CB2b the same way (exit_code stays 0 until a wait sets it) and fail now that the fork is real (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB-RM")
